    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build passes with all tests green. Here's a summary of the change I made:\n\n## Summary\n\nAdded a `timezone` field to the owner response, derived from the locality/region via the fixed table (NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney, VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne, QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane), returned as an IANA name.\n\n**Three edits:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `timezone` string property to the `OwnerDto` schema (right after `locality`), so the generated DTO carries the new field.\n\n2. **`Locality.java`** \u2014 added a `REGION_TIMEZONE` table and a `timezone(String region)` method that maps a region to its IANA name, returning `null` for an absent/unknown region (mirroring how `postcodeRange` handles unknown regions).\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `@Mapping` for `timezone` that feeds the region (already computed via `CustomerCode.region(owner.getCustomerCode())`) into `Locality.timezone(...)`, keeping it consistent with how `locality` is derived.\n\n**Test result:** `BUILD SUCCESS`, the acceptance test `coreReturnsTimezoneForRegion` (Sydney \u2192 NSW \u2192 `Australia/Sydney`) passes \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.\n\nI reused the existing region derivation rather than re-deriving from city/postcode, so the timezone stays coherent with the reported `locality` (both read off the same `customerCode` region), and an unknown region yields an absent `timezone` rather than an error.",
